-- Migration v6.8 — reliability anchored on published studies, brand by brand
--
-- The reliability score of every engine was a hand-set number with no stated
-- source. This migration ties it to what the public studies say about the
-- brand, and records which studies:
--
--   Euroconsumers / DECO PROteste survey 2026 (54 000 owners in 10 countries,
--     4 231 in Portugal; cars 6 months to 12 years old)             weight 3
--   What Car? Reliability Survey 2026 (UK, cars up to 5 years old)  weight 2
--   TÜV-Report 2026 (German inspections, older cars; 12 brands)     weight 2
--   Consumer Reports 2026 (US)                                      weight 1
--   J.D. Power Vehicle Dependability Study 2026 (US; partial)       weight 1
--   Auto Plus baromètre 2026 (France; rank only)                    weight 0.5
--
-- A brand's place in each study is turned into a percentile of the brands
-- that study ranks; the weighted mean, pulled towards the middle when few
-- studies cover the brand, is mapped onto the catalogue's scale (6.0 to 9.2).
-- Only our own composite and the list of studies are stored, not their tables.
--
-- brand_reliability gets one row per brand (35 with studies; Alpine, Maserati, Omoda
-- without: they keep their previous level and say so).
--
-- engines.reliability_score becomes: the brand's anchor, plus how far the
-- engine stood from its brand's average before, kept between -1.0 and +0.6.
-- That deviation is still editorial judgement (a plug-in below a petrol, an
-- engine with a known weakness below its siblings); the brand level no longer
-- is. engines.reliability_basis records it, and makes this run once per row.
--
-- Run after v67 (engines of the new models), so that they are anchored too.

BEGIN;

ALTER TABLE engines ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS reliability_basis VARCHAR(120);

INSERT INTO brand_reliability (brand_id, score, studies, sources, method, as_of)
SELECT b.id, v.score, v.studies, CAST(v.sources AS jsonb), v.method, DATE '2026-11-14'
